  "request": {
    "spec": "Release version 2 of the owner identity. Rederive the region code used INSIDE the identifiers, the householdId, the identityKey and the memberId with a version-2 algorithm that mixes in a fixed 'V2' version tag, so every identifier changes and no value produced under version 1 is produced again. The user-facing 'locality' field is NOT an identifier: it must remain the plain region code (for example 'NSW'), so the V2 tag only appears inside the identifiers, never in 'locality', 'timezone' or the owner segment's derived region. Group the memberId, identityKey and householdId under a nested 'identity' object in the owner response, and add a top-level integer 'apiVersion' of 2. Move the audit event to schema version 2 with an added 'schemaVersion' of 2, and recompute the owner segment from the version-2 identity. The request fields are unchanged. Several existing behaviours across the identity derivation, the response shape, the audit schema and the owner segment change at once.",
    "prompt": "Implement the following change to the application so that it fully satisfies the\nspecification and all existing and new tests pass.\n\nAs you do, keep the code well structured: put each piece of logic where it belongs, in a\nsmall unit with a single responsibility, and reuse existing code instead of copying it. Do\nnot let any one class or method grow into a catch-all that accumulates unrelated logic. Run\nthe test suite and make it green before finishing.\n\nRelease version 2 of the owner identity. Rederive the region code used INSIDE the identifiers, the householdId, the identityKey and the memberId with a version-2 algorithm that mixes in a fixed 'V2' version tag, so every identifier changes and no value produced under version 1 is produced again. The user-facing 'locality' field is NOT an identifier: it must remain the plain region code (for example 'NSW'), so the V2 tag only appears inside the identifiers, never in 'locality', 'timezone' or the owner segment's derived region. Group the memberId, identityKey and householdId under a nested 'identity' object in the owner response, and add a top-level integer 'apiVersion' of 2. Move the audit event to schema version 2 with an added 'schemaVersion' of 2, and recompute the owner segment from the version-2 identity. The request fields are unchanged. Several existing behaviours across the identity derivation, the response shape, the audit schema and the owner segment change at once.\n"
  },
